    "slug": "meds-by-mail-covered-medications",
    "title": "Which Medications Does CHAMPVA Meds by Mail Cover?",
    "primary_question": "Which medications and supplies can CHAMPVA beneficiaries receive through Meds by Mail, and how do I confirm current availability for a specific drug or strength?",
    "summary": "VA publishes a current Meds by Mail medication and supply list, but it is an example list rather than a complete permanent formulary. This reference explains how to check a specific medication, what a listing does and does not guarantee, and when to confirm the exact drug, strength, dosage form, or supply directly with Meds by Mail.",
    "body_markdown": "\n## Quick answer\n\nVA publishes a current list of medications and supplies available through Meds by Mail, but **the list is not complete and should not be treated as a permanent formulary**. VA specifically says that some medications or supplies may not appear on the page, some items shown may no longer be available, and Meds by Mail may not be able to provide every strength or supply item.\n\nThe safest way to check a prescription is to use VA's current [Meds by Mail medication and supply list](https://www.va.gov/resources/prescription-medications-covered-through-meds-by-mail/) and then confirm the exact medication, strength, dosage form, or supply with Meds by Mail when the page does not answer the question clearly.\n\nMeds by Mail generally provides generic prescription medications and certain brand-name medications. VA also says that certain controlled substances, including many opioid pain medications, are not available through Meds by Mail.\n\n*Last researched: September 21, 2026.*\n\n## How to check whether your medication is available\n\nUse this process instead of relying on an old article, screenshot, or saved drug list:\n\n1. **Open VA's current medication page.** The page is maintained by VA and was updated September 1, 2026, at the time of this review.\n2. **Search for the medication by brand name and generic name.** VA lists medications alphabetically by brand name when one exists and by generic name when there is no brand name.\n3. **Check the exact form and any notes.** A listing may specify tablets, capsules, injections, inhalers, eye drops, or another dosage form. Some entries also include special handling or coverage notes.\n4. **Confirm uncertain cases with Meds by Mail.** If the medication is missing, the strength is not shown, or you need a specific supply item, use the contact information on VA's current Meds by Mail page to confirm availability.\n\nA medication being absent from the example list does **not** by itself prove that Meds by Mail cannot provide it.\n\n## What a medication listing means—and what it does not mean\n\nA listing on VA's page is useful evidence that Meds by Mail commonly provides that medication or supply, but it is not a promise about every prescription.\n\nVA currently warns that:\n\n- the posted list contains common examples rather than every covered medication or supply;\n- some items may no longer be available even if they still appear on the page;\n- not every strength or supply item can necessarily be provided; and\n- Meds by Mail fills prescriptions with available generic or bioequivalent products, so a brand name shown for reference may not be the product you receive.\n\nThat is why the current VA page is more useful than copying the entire list into a third-party article. Medication availability can change faster than a static article can be kept current.\n\n## CHAMPVA coverage and Meds by Mail availability are not the same question\n\nThere are two separate issues:\n\n1. **Is the prescription covered under CHAMPVA's benefit rules?**\n2. **Can Meds by Mail supply that particular medication, strength, dosage form, or item?**\n\nFederal CHAMPVA rules provide benefits for medically necessary services and supplies that are not otherwise excluded, and they make clear that a prescription or recommendation alone does not automatically make an item payable. Medication-specific coverage restrictions can also apply.\n\nSeparately, VA's Meds by Mail guidance controls whether a covered prescription can be filled through the mail-order program.\n\nIf Meds by Mail cannot provide a medication, that does not automatically mean the medication is excluded from CHAMPVA altogether. Review the broader [CHAMPVA prescription drug coverage guide](https://valorwell.org/resources/champva/prescription-drug-coverage) for retail-pharmacy and other prescription routes.\n\n## Why you should recheck medication status before each new prescription\n\nMedication availability and coverage rules can change.\n\nVA's current medication page includes diagnosis-specific notes for some drugs and also identifies special shipping limitations for certain refrigerated products. These are exactly the kinds of details that can become outdated if someone relies on a saved list.\n\nBefore a new Meds by Mail prescription is sent, especially for a medication you have never received through the program, recheck the current VA page rather than assuming an older listing is still accurate.\n\n## Generic and brand-name medications\n\nVA says Meds by Mail covers **generic prescription medications and certain brand-name prescription medications**.\n\nThe medication list may display a familiar brand name to help you identify the drug, but VA says Meds by Mail may dispense an available generic or bioequivalent instead. If your prescriber believes a specific product is medically necessary, confirm how that prescription should be handled before relying on the brand name shown on the example list.\n\n## Controlled substances\n\nMeds by Mail does not provide every controlled substance. VA specifically states that certain controlled substances, including many opioid pain medications, are not covered through Meds by Mail.\n\nBecause controlled-substance rules are medication-specific, confirm the exact prescription with Meds by Mail rather than assuming that all controlled medications are either included or excluded.\n\n## Refrigerated medications and supplies\n\nThe current VA list includes some refrigerated medications and many supply items, but availability and shipping rules matter.\n\nVA says insulin and other refrigerated medications cannot be mailed to a PO box. Refrigerated medications also have geographic shipping limitations. The medication page separately warns that not every supply item or strength is necessarily available.\n\nFor supplies, check the exact product rather than assuming that one listed item means every version of that supply is available.\n\n## If your medication is not on the list\n\nDo not treat an absent listing as a denial.\n\nInstead:\n\n1. check both the brand and generic names;\n2. confirm the exact strength and dosage form;\n3. use VA's current Meds by Mail contact information to ask whether the item can be supplied; and\n4. if Meds by Mail cannot provide it, review whether the prescription can be filled through CHAMPVA's retail-pharmacy benefit.\n\nIf you are starting a medication through mail order for the first time, see [How to Start a New CHAMPVA Meds by Mail Prescription](https://valorwell.org/resources/champva/start-meds-by-mail-prescription). If you already receive it through Meds by Mail, see [How to Refill or Renew a CHAMPVA Meds by Mail Prescription](https://valorwell.org/resources/champva/refill-renew-meds-by-mail-prescription).\n\n## The practical rule\n\nUse VA's medication page as a **current checking tool**, not as a permanent downloadable formulary.\n\nFor a specific prescription, the most reliable sequence is:\n\n**current VA list → exact drug/strength/form check → Meds by Mail confirmation when unclear → retail-pharmacy review if mail order cannot supply it.**\n\nThat approach reduces the risk of relying on a medication status that has changed since an older guide was published.\n",
